#
# A SEPARATE, LATER-FOUND DEFECT (SIGBUS), ROOT-CAUSED AND FIXED - measured,
# not narrated: an on-card run of the served model (coder, u8:i4,
# PAGED_ATTENTION_MAX_PARTITIONS=32, chunk 128) crashed with SIGBUS during
# prefill of long prompts, reproduced on two different cards, 3/3, at
# depths corresponding to S >= 6-9 (raw partition count in the hundreds);
# 35k tokens (S=5) did not crash, 40k/120k-token device tests (S=5, S=15)
# did not crash either - only the served path, at depth, crashed. Running
# the identical failing case under MALLOC_CHECK_=3 MALLOC_PERTURB_=165
# made the crash disappear (prefill completed, decode ran normally) - the
# textbook signature of a use-after-free (or read of freed/uninitialised
# memory) whose victim depends on heap layout, not of an index-arithmetic
# bug (which perturbing free'd memory would not fix).
#
# Root cause, traced to source: `primitive_inst::realloc_intermediates()`
# (primitive_inst.cpp, generic framework code shared by every primitive
# with intermediate/scratch buffers - not part of this patch) reallocates
# or reinterprets each intermediate buffer's underlying memory object
# without ever setting `ExecutionFlags::MEMORY_CHANGED` or
# `ARG_UPDATE_REQUIRED`. The only thing that makes `execute_stage()`
# rebind a kernel's arguments (`primitive_ocl_base.hpp`'s
# `stage.kd.need_args_update`, gated on exactly those two flags) is a
# change to the primitive's OUTPUT memory, `has_mutable_input()`,
# `is_output()`, dynamic dependencies, or a full impl swap - never a pure
# intermediate-buffer identity change. Before this patch, paged
# attention's tmp_out buffer (buffer 7) grew every MIXED-stage chunk of a
# long prefill without bound, so its identity changed on effectively every
# call regardless; this patch is what makes tmp_out's required size
# plateau once PAGED_ATTENTION_MAX_PARTITIONS bounds it, so a genuine
# reallocation (a real new USM allocation, not `engine.reinterpret_buffer`'s
# same-handle new wrapper) can now occur at some later, size-crossing point
# deep into a long prefill with nothing else on that same call happening
# to also touch outputs/mutable-inputs/impl - the one existing path that
# would have forced a rebind. Once that reallocation's old memory object's
# last reference drops and its destructor frees the old allocation, a
# kernel still bound (via a stale OpenCL argument binding never refreshed)
# to that now-freed handle is a genuine use-after-free - matching the
# MALLOC_CHECK_/PERTURB_ result exactly. This task could not establish
# from static reading alone why the effect is sharpest around S=6-9
# specifically rather than at the first plateau (L=8,193) - that depends
# on real allocator/heap-layout behaviour not visible from source; the
# mechanism itself is verified from source, not guessed from the symptom.
#
# FIX A (in this patch, paged-attention-local): `PagedAttentionOptImpl`
# now tracks the identity (raw pointer) of every intermediate memory
# object its kernel arguments were last bound against
# (`m_last_seen_intermediates`). At the top of `execute()` - which, in the
# caller (`primitive_inst::execute()`), always runs AFTER
# `realloc_if_needed()`/`realloc_intermediates()` for this call, so
# `instance.get_intermediates_memories()` at this point already reflects
# whatever reallocation just happened - it compares the current
# intermediates against that record; on any difference, it forces
# `need_args_update = true` on every registered stage (cheap: a pointer
# comparison per intermediate on the common no-change path, no allocation)
# before any stage is dispatched. This placement matters: doing the same
# check inside `update()` (the MIXED-stage-only hook `requires_update()`
# gates) would not work - `update()` runs BEFORE `realloc_if_needed()` in
# the caller, so it would only ever see the PRE-reallocation identity, one
# call too early to detect what the reallocation that follows is about to
# change. Deliberately does not try to distinguish `reinterpret_buffer`'s
# same-handle case (very likely already harmless - it explicitly reuses
# the old handle and shares its mem_tracker) from a genuine reallocation
# (the actually dangerous one) - telling them apart cheaply from inside
# this primitive risks getting the direction wrong; comparing raw identity
# is simple, cheap, and conservative in the safe direction for both.
#
# RESIDUAL, STATED ON REVIEW AND SINCE CONFIRMED BY MEASUREMENT (not
# speculative any more - see below): fix A repairs the BINDING (which
# memory object a future kernel dispatch is bound to), not the LIFETIME
# (whether an already-in-flight GPU kernel enqueued against the OLD
# memory object is guaranteed to have completed before that object's last
# reference drops and its destructor can free it). `prepare_internal_
# buffers`'s `mem_lock` on `past_lens` for the MIXED-stage path provides
# an incidental synchronisation point for THAT buffer, not a deliberate
# guarantee for buffers 5/6/7 specifically; the GENERATE-without-scores
# path takes an early return in that same function and has no such
# barrier at all. This is not fixed by fix A and is recorded here as a
# residual for item B - properly closing it likely needs the framework's
# own memory-pool/dependency-tracking (event completion, not just identity
# bookkeeping) to extend an "old" intermediate's lifetime until whatever
# last referenced it has signalled done, which is squarely framework
# scope, not something to improvise locally.
#
# ITEM B, RECORDED, NOT FIXED HERE - DESIGN BACKLOG: the gap in
# `primitive_inst::realloc_intermediates()` itself (never setting
# MEMORY_CHANGED/ARG_UPDATE_REQUIRED on ANY intermediate-buffer identity
# change, for ANY primitive) is a pre-existing defect in shared framework
# code that this patch is the first to expose in practice, by being the
# first code path in this specific primitive whose intermediate buffer
# size profile plateaus after growing rather than growing forever. It is
# NOT fixed in this patch - fixing it at the framework level would change
# behaviour for every primitive with intermediate buffers in this
# codebase, not just paged attention, and deserves its own dedicated
# review rather than riding in bundled with a paged-attention-specific
# patch. Candidate framework-level fix (not implemented, not built): have
# `realloc_intermediates()` set `ExecutionFlags::MEMORY_CHANGED` (or
# `ARG_UPDATE_REQUIRED` directly) whenever it actually replaces an
# intermediate buffer's identity, in either branch (reuse-via-reinterpret
# or genuine reallocation). Upstream-worthy on its own.
#
# FIX A MEASURED INSUFFICIENT ON ITS OWN - the residual above is not
# theoretical. A follow-up on-card round found: the 56,348-token cell
# passed once with fix A, but the 119,074-token cell (P_MAX=32, chunk 128,
# on a card with a smaller VRAM budget) crashed again with a bus error;
# separately, on a different, larger-VRAM card at chunk 2048, a 37k-token
# prompt crashed after an
# 8.4k-token prompt had passed - within roughly a dozen forward calls, far
# fewer than the several hundred this task's own S=6-9 threshold estimate
# (at chunk 128) implied were needed for the effect to appear. That last
# point matters on its own: it weighs against "accumulates over many
# repeated calls" as the primary mechanism and toward something tied to a
# specific transition or boundary instead - consistent with, though not
# proof of, the lifetime residual just above (an unlucky race can happen
# on the very first post-plateau reallocation, not only after many).
#
